## How to choose a recruitment SEO agency or consultant in the UK: 8 criteria

Choose a recruitment SEO agency or consultant in the UK against these eight criteria. They apply whether you hire an independent consultant like me or a larger firm.

1. **They ask about your revenue model first.** Permanent, contract and executive search firms need different pages. A good provider asks which desks and sectors make you money before suggesting keywords.
2. **They understand job-page indexing.** Ask how they would handle expired jobs, duplicate jobs and Google for Jobs markup. Vague answers are a warning sign.
3. **They check your job board platform's output.** Many recruitment sites publish jobs through a CRM or job feed. The provider should test what that platform actually produces.
4. **They separate candidate and employer work.** One plan should cover both. Each side needs its own pages and measures.
5. **They avoid doorway pages.** Hundreds of near-identical "jobs in [town]" pages are a risk. Ask how they would make each location page useful.
6. **They measure enquiries as well as rankings.** Applications, CV uploads and employer briefs should be tracked separately.
7. **They are honest about AI search.** Nobody can guarantee your firm appears in ChatGPT or Google's AI Overviews. A provider who promises it is overselling.
8. **Prices and scope are written down.** You should know the fee, what is included and what you need to supply before work starts.

My guide on [how to choose an SEO consultant](/blog/how-to-choose-seo-consultant/) covers the general questions in more detail.

## How do job pages get into Google for Jobs?

Job pages get into Google for Jobs when they carry valid JobPosting structured data. Google then shows them in a dedicated job search feature.

Google documents the fields in its [JobPosting structured data](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/job-posting) guide. The markup describes the title, location, salary, employment type and closing date. It must match what the visible page says.

Expired jobs are where many recruitment sites go wrong. Google's guidance is to remove expired listings from the feature. This is the sequence I set up on a recruitment site:

{% process %}
{% step title="Set validThrough at publish" detail="Every live job carries its closing date in the markup so the listing ends on time even if nobody touches the page." /%}
{% step title="Expire the page on close" detail="The day a role is filled or withdrawn the page returns 404 or 410. A page that must stay live loses its JobPosting markup instead." /%}
{% step title="Update the sitemap" detail="Closed jobs leave the XML sitemap so crawling goes to live roles rather than to pages nobody needs." /%}
{% step title="Check a sample monthly" detail="Expired URLs are tested for soft 404s and stale markup. Problems usually point to the job feed or the CMS template." /%}
{% /process %}

Avoid leaving thousands of closed jobs live with a "this role has been filled" message. Google may treat them as soft 404s. They also waste crawling on pages nobody needs. The technical SEO audit checks a sample of live and expired jobs for these problems.

## How do you build sector and location pages without doorway pages?

Build each sector and location page around real jobs, real consultants and real local knowledge. Sector and location pages are how recruiters rank for searches like "accountancy recruitment Reading" or "IT contract jobs Manchester". They work when each page has a real reason to exist.

A useful page includes live jobs filtered to that sector or place, the consultants who cover it, salary or rate guidance you can support and local employer context. A page that only swaps the town name in a template is a doorway page. Google's [spam policies on doorway abuse](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies#doorways) cover this pattern.

Start with the sectors and areas where you already place people. Add more pages only when you have the jobs and the knowledge to fill them.

## Why split candidate and employer intent?

Split candidate and employer intent because the two audiences search differently and want different outcomes from a recruitment site. Candidates want live roles. Employers want proof you can fill one.

{% panels %}
{% panel eyebrow="Candidate intent" title="Example: marketing jobs Reading" %}
- **What they want:** live roles and an easy way to apply
- **Best page type:** job listings and sector job pages
- **What to measure:** applications and CV uploads
- **Who builds it:** the job feed builds these pages automatically
{% /panel %}
{% panel eyebrow="Employer intent" title="Example: marketing recruitment agency Reading" %}
- **What they want:** proof you can fill a role, fees and process
- **Best page type:** sector service pages, hire-staff pages, fees and process pages
- **What to measure:** employer briefs and calls
- **Who builds it:** you have to build these pages deliberately
{% /panel %}
{% /panels %}

Many recruitment sites put most of their effort into candidate pages because the job feed creates them automatically. Employer pages bring in the revenue. They need deliberate content.

## Do AI assistants recommend recruitment agencies?

Yes. AI assistants sometimes recommend recruitment agencies. Some hiring managers now ask ChatGPT, Copilot and Perplexity for recruiter shortlists. These tools draw on pages they can read. Those pages include your site, directories, trade press and review platforms.

I am cautious here. AI answers change from one run to the next. No one can guarantee a mention. What you can do is make your firm's sectors, locations and specialisms clear and consistent everywhere they appear. AEO for a recruitment or staffing agency should start with a measured baseline. My [AI visibility audit](/ai-visibility/) checks agreed employer questions across the available AI search tools and benchmarks your firm against three agreed competitors for £1,500 fixed.

## What about job board platforms and recruitment CRMs?

Recruitment platforms and CRMs often decide how a recruitment site's job pages are built. Examples include Bullhorn, Vincere and Broadbean or a website plugin that pulls jobs from them. Their output sets URLs, page titles, structured data and what happens when a job closes.

I do not assume any platform gets this right or wrong. I check the actual output on your site: the HTML Google receives, the markup, the status codes on expired jobs and the sitemap. Fixes may sit with your web developer or the platform's support team.

